From ed900f45cca55750f2c1ffb9993c66de1890e270 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 11:28:15 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 3/3] Scope every API router to the authenticated patient; fix
 IDOR gaps found along the way
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Every endpoint that reads or writes patient data now depends on
get_current_patient_id instead of assuming a single implicit patient:

- analysis.py, chat.py, nutrition.py: straightforward scoping, analyzers now
  built per-request (see previous commit).
- manual_entry.py: same, plus fixes update_family_member, delete_family_member
  and delete_health_record, which looked up by bare id with no ownership
  check — any authenticated user could edit or delete another patient's row
  by guessing/incrementing its id. Now filtered by (id, patient_id).
  delete_nutrition_entry in nutrition.py had the same gap, fixed the same way.
- upload.py: OCR-extracted metrics now save with the uploading patient's id —
  app/ocr/data_extractor.py previously hardcoded patient_id=1 for every
  extracted record, regardless of who uploaded the document. index_document's
  call site updated for its new patient_id parameter. /documents (raw
  filenames on the shared container disk) restricted to admins.
- apple_health.py: patient_id=1 was hardcoded here too, and the Apple Health
  dedup check (and every read/stats/delete endpoint) queried across all
  patients with no filter at all — DELETE /data was an unconditional
  `DELETE FROM apple_health_data`, deletable by any authenticated user for
  every patient. All fixed; background-import jobs now record and check the
  owning patient_id so /import-status can't be polled by anyone else.
- predictions.py: dropped the module-level RiskPredictor() singleton (same
  cross-tenant risk as TrendAnalyzer/RAG in the previous commit); ~
  RecommendationEngine/MedicalAdvisor stay shared since they hold no patient
  data of their own.
- integrations.py: Garmin, Withings and Google Calendar each hold one
  process-wide OAuth session, not one per user — turning that into real
  multi-tenancy (a token per patient) is a separate, larger piece of work.
  For this pass every endpoint that reads or syncs through them requires
  require_admin (ADMIN_EMAILS), instead of either ripping the integrations
  out or leaving them reachable by any tester. withings_sync.py's
  sync_withings_to_db now takes the calling admin's own patient_id rather
  than "whichever Patient row sorts first", which mattered once ADMIN_EMAILS
  can list more than one address.

New tests/test_multi_tenant_isolation.py is the one this branch exists for:
two independently registered accounts, checked both through normal list
endpoints (B must never see A's rows) and through the id-based endpoints
(B must not read/edit/delete A's rows by id — the IDOR class fixed above).
tests/test_auth.py covers register/login/logout/me and the rate limiter.

Known limitation, disclosed rather than hidden: this sandbox has no PyPI
access, so fastapi/sqlalchemy/anthropic could not be installed and none of
this — old or new — could actually be run here. Every changed file was
syntax-checked with `python3 -m py_compile`; the real test suite still needs
to run in CI or locally before this merges to master.
